      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Stress has been shown capable of differentially activating opioid- and non-opioid-mediated endogenous analgesia systems. In this study, the muscarinic cholinergic antagonist, scopolamine, but not the centrally inactive methylscopolamine, blocks opioid, but not non-opioid stress analgesia. Additionally, naltrexone, an opiate antagonist, attenuates analgesia induced by oxotremorine, a cholinergic agonist. These findings support the existence of a muscarinic cholinergic synapse in a central nervous system opioid pain-inhibitory pathway."},
